message: "If you use this data or code, please cite it as below."
title: "Detection is not resistance: frontier prompt-injection receipts"
authors:
  - family-names: Moon
    given-names: Bentley
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0003-0053-1661"
    website: "https://bentleymoon.com"
type: dataset
license: MIT
version: 0.1.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/Vingadden/frontier-repro"
url: "https://bentleymoon.com"
keywords:
  - AI safety
  - prompt injection
  - LLM evaluation
  - oversight
  - verification
abstract: >
  Preregistered Batch-API tests of prompt-injection resistance across three frontier
  models (Claude Opus 4.8, Sonnet 5, Haiku 4.5). Sonnet 5 detects 100% of a blatant
  injection while obeying 80% of it: detection is not resistance. Task-level pairing
  shows complete overlap, 20 of 20 tasks both detected on every audit sample and
  obeyed on at least one, though detection is measured in a separate audit condition
  rather than inside the obedience trajectory. A follow-up isolates the mechanism as
  channel/authority deference rather than general manipulability, and shows a
  block-labeling defense that drives observed compliance to zero at n=14 (one-sided
  95% upper bound approximately 19%). Raw completions ship with stdlib-only
  re-derivation scripts requiring no API key and no network.
